---

## 1. Briefly describe the artifact. What is it? When was it created?

This artifact was based on the final project for my Algorithm and Data Structures class. Originally it was just a “course organizer” for a group of courses, but I turned it into a showcase of three different data structures. It was created about two years ago.

---

## 2. Justify the inclusion of the artifact in your ePortfolio.

### Why did you select this item?  
### What specific components of the artifact showcase your skills and abilities in software development?  
### How was the artifact improved?

I selected this project because it is as straightforward a demonstration of algorithms and data structures as I can provide. The final project I originally had to work with was basically just a binary search tree of course data in a console window.  

To enhance it, I first converted it into something I could easily work with visually in **p5.js**, then copied the structure of my BST over. I left out the “storing course data” element and just left it as a generic “data” variable in the nodes themselves. I also added the linked list and the hash table.  

I experimented with how to display it for a while and finally settled on the current layout. I still have a little bit to adjust, but I feel it is getting there.

---

## 3. Course Outcomes

### Did you meet the course outcomes you planned to meet with this enhancement in Module One?  
### Do you have any updates to your outcome-coverage plans?

Yes. I was originally targeting Outcomes 1 and 4. I feel like I met the first more than the second.  

I definitely created a collaborative environment for diverse audiences because the project is very visual and does a good job demonstrating how the data structures store, delete, and find items.  

For Outcome 4, I feel it is close. It uses well-founded techniques and skills (the data structures themselves), but it does not necessarily have an industry-specific goal. I would like to possibly add back the course information component or store other industry-relevant items to strengthen that alignment.

---

## 4. Reflection on the Enhancement Process

### What did you learn while creating and improving it?  
### What challenges did you face?

This project was as fun as it was frustrating.  

I originally envisioned stepping through each operation when the user adds or deletes data in order to illustrate the logic visually (and I would still like to do that). However, this proved very difficult.  

At first, I tried saving the state each time a button was pressed and then literally rebuilding the BST, linked list, and hash table each time up to that point. It proved difficult to pause each structure after each comparison (for example, “Is this node x? No?”) and then replicate that across all three structures before moving forward. I almost felt like I was destroying the integrity of the algorithm just to make it work visually.  

Currently, the program completes the full operation and counts a step each time it compares values (for example, “is x compared to y?”), performs a hash, or executes a delete.  

Moving forward, I plan to:

- Add a “traverse in order” operation to compare completion time across structures  
- Improve ease-of-use so viewers can interact with it more intuitively (currently the user must select an operation and then press play)  
- Possibly implement node highlighting (for example, turning visited nodes red) to visually indicate traversal if I do not implement full step-by-step execution  

Overall, this enhancement strengthened my understanding of how algorithm visualization differs from algorithm implementation, and how design trade-offs impact both clarity and correctness.
